Puppet configuration for Holgerspexet

Runs with OpenVox (the community fork of Puppet) via `puppet apply`,
driven by a hourly cronjob (puppetfetch). No puppet master, no hiera.

## Nodes

* `insidan.holgerspexet.se` - production insidan server (upgraded
  18.04 -> 26.04)
* `holgerlabb.hx.ax` - temporary test server, runs the whole insidan
  stack; deleted once the migration is done
* `holgerspexet-public` - production public/wordpress server
  (a separate machine)

## Bootstrap a new node

1. Install OpenVox. Enable the repository and install the agent:
   ```
   wget https://apt.voxpupuli.org/openvox8-release-ubuntu26.04.deb
   dpkg -i openvox8-release-ubuntu26.04.deb
   apt update && apt install openvox-agent
   ```
   (binaries end up in /opt/puppetlabs/bin, same as puppet always had)

2. Make sure the machine has its proper fqdn (hostname + /etc/hosts).
   Everything else takes its name from the `networking.fqdn` fact.

3. Clone this repo to /opt/holger-puppet, add the deploy keys needed
   for the private repos (holger-puppet, holger-archive, grunkor), and
   apply once by hand:
   ```
   cd /opt/holger-puppet
   git submodule update --init
   /opt/puppetlabs/bin/puppet apply --modulepath=/opt/holger-puppet/modules/ \
       /opt/holger-puppet/manifests/site.pp
   ```
   The cronjob (`puppetfetcher`, hour :55) takes over from there.

4. Notes:
   * The first apply bootstraps the letsencrypt certificate; nginx will
     not start before the cert exists, that is expected.
   * OpenProject runs as a podman-compose stack in /opt/openproject.
     The first boot takes a few minutes (db migrations).
   * If a private git repo has no deploy key on the machine its puppet
     run fails until a key is added. Everything else is unaffected.
   * ssh authorized keys are NOT managed here. Never add
     `purge_ssh_keys` to the root user - the keys are not in this
     repository and purging locks everyone out.

## Upgrading OpenProject (deb -> compose)

See disaster-recovery.md, section about OpenProject.
